--TEST--
`Cli\Text + Cli\Text` is `Cli\Text`, so the sum is still the sink's one raw path — ADR 0086 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2: "`Text + Text` is `Text`, immutable (R20), composing the way `Markup`
// already does". Neither operand is substituted a second time on the way in —
// each was neutralized by the constructor that built it, and the Control
// Picture below is what a second pass would have nothing left to do to.
var $esc = Core\Cli\Text::plain(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(27) . "[31m");
var $word = Core\Cli\Text::plain("red");
echo $esc + $word, "\n";

// The sum is a `Cli\Text` and not merely bytes that print the same: the
// declared type asserts it, and composing the result again is what would stop
// compiling if `+` answered a `string`.
Core\Cli\Text $whole = $esc + $word;
echo $whole + $whole, "\n";

// Still the carrier, which is the whole point: `Core\Cli::write` takes a
// `string|Cli\Text` and writes a `Text` through unchanged (§ 1's one raw
// path), so the sum reaches the sink as the class rather than as its bytes.
Core\Cli::write($whole);
echo "\n";

// A `Text` is a value: `+` builds a third one rather than growing the left
// operand, so both read back exactly as they were written.
echo $esc, "|", $word, "\n";
--EXPECT--
␛[31mred
␛[31mred␛[31mred
␛[31mred
␛[31m|red
